A||||13||Dingo||Fiero or Porsche||||10||
Z||000000||Dingo||03-26-2001||04:41 AM||||I used to own a 1985 Fiero SE6. Nice car overall. Handled rather well. Only problem I had was the time I drove it home had cheap tires. Grandam. In the rain it was real easy to lose traction and spin out or lose it on the highway. After I replaced the tires that problem pretty much went away. Anyway it has been a few years and I want to get into another project car. Right now I am looking at buying an 1985 Porsche 944. I intended on replacing the front fenders/hood/etc. and rear 1/4's with fiberglass and go wider front and rear about 2 inches. Then pull the motor and replace it with a V8 kit from Renegade. The tranny will also be rebuilt to handle and also match up to the V8 with different gears. I know this car would be real kick a$$ after I am finished with it. Car originally is about 2700 pounds. The car is almost 50/50 weight distribution. I estimate an 11 sec. in the 1/4 and a top speed of 170 to 180 with the new gears. Not that I ever intend on going that fast. But I do get tired of the rice burners in their hundas and toyotas, etc. trying run around town like they are in a formula one car. A 1966/67 Chevy II was 2600 pounds and from the factory with the 327 could turn an easy 13.5 in the 1/4 and with ladderbars, etc was able to go below 12's easy. I used to own a 1966 Chevy II.<P>Now that I have all that out of the way I wanted to ask the list about another option I have been thinking about. <BR>Can I get similiar performance from a Fiero? When I owned my Fiero people were not that far along with the V8 kits and other upgrades. I'm not to sure about placing a V8 in the Fiero as I would think it would make it even that much more heavy in the rear. So what can I expect in todays Fiero mods when it comes to racing and what kinda of weight distribution can I expect? If I'm going to spend the money I expect a car that will perform and kick the living crap out of them rice cars.||||reg||10
Z||000001||Wolfhound||03-26-2001||05:06 AM||||I,m curious as to what weight distribution you will have with a V8 hanging behind the rear axle of a porsche.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Nashco||03-26-2001||06:57 AM||||Wolfhound, 944's are front engined.  The 944's and 924's have a pretty bad reputation with Porsche enthusiasts, but they've still got some things going for them.  I think that this swap would be just as much of a squeeze, if not more, than our little fieros, wouldn't it Dingo?  I'm sure Dingo's seen the engine bay, but check this out:  <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=572673096" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/ebaymotors/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=572673096</A> <P>That brake booster would be a pain, and it looks like you'd have a fun time stripping everything out, getting all new components, and finding a hole to stuff them into.<P>Just food for thought, hope things work out for ya...your idea isn't too much different than a v8 vega or pinto though.  The only difference is the uniqueness, and about 200 hours <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000003||Wolfhound||03-26-2001||07:16 AM||||Duh, I,ve got one sitting in my yard. Too dang early must have misread the post.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000004||firstfiero||03-26-2001||07:38 AM||||   God I miss my 924...had to get rid of it when I got my divorce. But I think I love my fiero even more!  <P>------------------<BR>'86 fiero Se<BR>    Todd<BR>NO MR. RICER STICKERS DON'T MAKE YOU FASTER!!!||||reg||9
Z||000005||Cheever2||03-26-2001||09:45 AM||||Dingo, there's many people here with more knowledge at this than I, but everything I've read indicates there's not much if any weight change in a Fiero going from V6 to V8.<BR>||||reg||6
Z||000006||Oreif||03-26-2001||10:45 AM||||The Porsche 944 has 50/50 distribution, But once you put in a V-8 that will change. Even the 3rd gen F-bodies were 50/50 distribution with the 2.8L v-6 but once the V-8 went in that changed. The Fiero engine goes in the middle, In front of the rear axles, so your weight is still centered.<BR>With the Fiero, You also have a large choice of swaps. A 3800 SC in a Fiero will toast most V-8 cars. As for V-8's you can put in a SB chevy and squeeze over 400hp (expensive but can be done). I don't know what the cost of a V-8 runs for a 944, But a V-8 Fiero swap can range from $2000 to $10,000 (if you have the work done). Whichever way you go, I'm sure you won't have any problems with the local ricers.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Phoenix||03-26-2001||01:42 PM||||Dingo -<BR>If you want a real killer, skip the 944 and look for a 914.  I'm doing a Renegade kit on a 71 914 chassis right now.  With the correct selection of block/heads the original weight distribution can be maintained for outstanding handling and with a sbc @ ~250 to 300 hp it will stomp on just about anything on the street  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dingo||03-26-2001||06:09 PM||||The Porsche 944 was not very welcomed in a rear engine 911 body style community. Your average traditionalists is not very found of the 944 or 951. Fact is the later 1980's 951 or 944 Turbo could out perform these guys and had a higher top end. The 4 cylindr is huge and heavy. With the V8 swap it actually is closer to a 50/50 ratio over the factory setup. The Chevy V8 fits very well in the 944 engine bay. With the added options of fiberglass fenders/hood/quarter panels and these same parts can be bought in carbonfiber for an additional 100 to 250 more I'd say the weight distribution can be brought to a 50/50 or close to that, and make the car lighter.<P>I am still keeping the Fiero in mind. I would like to know how a Fiero can be made to perform like the 951. I would enjoy either car but need more information before taking on the Fiero over the 944. Right now I have not made any purchase. I have the 944 on hold. Going to pick it up for a real cheap price of less then 900 USD. I expect race car like performance. In and out of corners and power to boot. <P>btw...anyone remember a company called Pegisus Engineering out of Oregon?<P>thanks||||reg||6
